 {:seon.task/name "a-flush-costs-the-children-that-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "From the managing session's audit (2026-11-10, per-turn costs): a flush of a list component walks every child it holds, cache hits included, so one changed child costs the list O(children): measured 27k ticks at 10 children, 326k at 100 (seon.render/flush! and seon.render/emit's cache-hit path, a lookup a member). It should cost the children that changed plus O(1) for the list. Learn first: what jolt/incr (retired) measured and its delta-interface notes, and seon.render/render!'s hit path. Proof, red first: a cost proof that grows the unchanged children (10, 40, 160) with one child changed, ticks flat within its bound. Measured 2026-11-10 (Web, seon.render-cost-test/a-flush-costs-the-children-that-changed, on jolt/walk-2a): one member's value changed costs flat calls, 81 at 10, 40 and 160 members, and runs that member alone (with a member keyed by entity in a collection sorted by ids, as ctx holds a join; a plain map or vector is compared whole by seon.ctx/changes and reruns every reader), so flush! already costs what changed; bytes grow about 0.8 KB a member from seon.ctx/changes stepping past each member of the collection it enters. The per-member term the audit measured is the list's own rerun when its membership changes (a new eval or turn): the list render builds every member's call and emit looks up each marker, 7 calls and about 4.2 KB a member (seon.render-cost-test/a-member-gone-costs-the-lists-lookups). Making that O(1) is a new mechanism (an append-aware list, or a list's emitted output patched by its moved members), so it waits on a design the owner approves."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-list-costs-the-members-that-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "Held for the owner's choice after stage 2b lands (managing session, 2026-11-10: a new mechanism inside pull -> ratom -> render, which the owner said not to change, and it changes the shape 2b's model renders read; to be brought as one priced choice with a real turn at 100 evals measured beside it). What is wrong: a list component whose members change (a new eval, turn or message) runs its render again, which builds every member's call, and seon.render/emit looks up each earlier member's marker: 7 calls and about 4.2 KB a member (seon.render-cost-test/a-member-gone-costs-the-lists-lookups); the audit of 2026-11-10 measured a turn's render 27k ticks at 10 evals and 326k at 100. One member's value changed is already flat (seon.render-cost-test/a-flush-costs-the-children-that-changed: 81 calls at 10, 40 and 160). The design: a collection view (a join's map sorted by member ids, each member an entity) placed in hiccup is rendered by the walk as its members, each its entity's component keyed by its identity (seon.render/entity-render); the collection is a component whose output is patched by the :add and :remove ops seon.ctx/changes already answers (the ratom's :keys reads say which collection), so a member added costs one component and an O(log n) insert. A list render returns the collection instead of looping over it (conversation-list's turns, a turn's evals and messages). Reuses the ratom's :keys tracking, seon.ctx/changes' ops, entity-render and the walk's cache. Deletes: each list render's loop over its members, emit's per-member :forms lookup on a list's rerun, and :seon.render/list ids where a collection's own key names its container. Cost line: n = members; today about 7 calls and 4.2 KB a member a membership change, after about 81 calls flat (one member's component and the collection's patch); a real turn at 100 evals from about 326k ticks to near one component's 11-18k. Proof: seon.render-cost-test/a-member-gone-costs-the-lists-lookups becomes :growth :constant; the page and the model's messages for a turn unchanged byte for byte (test/seon/recorded_requests.edn), reviewed with Context."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-hiccup-is-a-schema-checked-intermediate"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-10 (yes to Web's proposal, schema first, after stage 2b lands): \"How can we make the intermediate hiccup a better representation that you can render into html and context can render into the openai-compat format?\"; and \"any eval that has a value that we lookup a render for we show inline if the user expands it but also automatically add in the tile. Agents will see all the tiles\". With Context (the model's reader); docs/render.md#what-the-agent-reads-is-its-native-history-and-everything-else-through-the-hiccup.
The representation: (1) every component is a call headed by the render that made it ([seon.render.ui/task {:key id} ...]), so an agent knows what to edit; (2) inside it only semantic elements (:section :h2 :p :ul :li :code :pre :a :details), no :class or :style: the Tailwind strings (about 255) move to CSS keyed on render names, the html reader adding data-render; (3) a component's :key is its entity's identity value (2a's entity-render); (4) a value with a render is a call of that render with the value, expanded by the html reader; the model's reader keeps conversation entities (eval, message, reply) unexpanded and prints the rest; (5) one Malli hiccup schema (heads a render var or an element keyword, attributes unqualified keys only) that render outputs are checked against, so styling or an entity map in attributes is refused where it is written (lab 2026-11-09: a page validated in about 1 ms); (6) two readers, the html reader and Context's <page> reader; the :wire/* marks and seon.web.html's strip go.
Also here, with F: the eval tiles as stated above.
What the schema guarantees the model's page reader (Context, 2026-11-10): a component is [render-sym {:key k} & children], its head the render's qualified symbol (never a var or fn); :key the entity's identity value (or the render's own id), unique among siblings and stable across renders, so a diff's paths are keys; children vectors only (a seq the render returns is made a vector by the walk, nils dropped, markers resolved), in a stable order; nothing time-varying inside a part (a clock-derived value is refused by the check); a call's entity is known from its head (the render named on that entity's schema) and :key, so the reader leaves unexpanded any call whose entity the model reads natively (eval, message, model call). Eval tiles (owner, 2026-11-10, overruling the unexpanded reading: \"If there's a value render function then it's not the same as the eval value ... the rendered tile is the only version of the rendered value that is seen by the model. So yes I want the real value in the evals and rendered values int he tiles\"; \"It's expanded in the tile section which agents should be seeing as it minus the css ... If the user expands the UI > for the evals then yeah render it inline too but the agent doesn't need to see that AND a duplicate tile. The tiles are a way for the user to see what the model is seeing with any rendered data.\"): the eval's tool result keeps the real value; a value with a render is a tile, a part of its own in agent-parts, expanded in the hiccup the model reads; when a person opens the eval in the conversation the html may render the value inline too, html only, never the model's; only the conversation entities' own display (eval, message, model call: a schema naming an openai-compat render) stays an unexpanded call for the model. Cost: a tile is in every diff where its render's output changes; n = tiles on the page. Where the check runs (Point, 2026-11-10, from Deep fixes' measure of malli walking every element): at a render's definition and in its tests (a render's output on its sample data fits the schema; a law lists every hiccup render with no such test), and in the walk only for the one component that just ran, over its own output with its children as markers, never the whole tree per flush and never as a contract on a hot inner function. Cost line: n = the elements of one component's own output (children are markers, not walked); O(n) per component run, expected under 50 elements and a few thousand ticks for a typical part, against the component's render itself. Order: the schema and its check first (makes every violation visible), then styling moved to CSS a render at a time, then the strip and the marks deleted. Proofs: every render's output in the test world validates against the hiccup schema; a page's html unchanged in content (classes aside); the model's page message holds no class or style; an eval with a rendered value appears as a tile in agent-parts and in the model's page message."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-user-agent-renders-the-users-view"
  :seon.task/plan "Stage 4 of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09, Web's part (Optimization's: the user agent's lifecycle and branch). Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I want the user to have a process regardless. Anything that's user focused should be handled by this agent too so separate users don't take down the system\"; \"it's a read only ctx tree. The user isn't modifying anything it's sending the requests to agents\"; \"root I think is mostly the go between for flow communication\".

The change: the user agent's walk of its pull (itself, root, the agents it started: :seon.agent/agents, their main half) is the person's whole view, drawn by the user's render (its home: root's chat in the middle and the session tiles beside it, owner 2026-11-08). For each agent its render shows whole, it asks that agent {:op :render :since t} and holds the answers in a ratom of its own beside @ctx, diffed as ctx is (seon.ctx/changes) and read through a view, so a component reruns on its own agent's paths; two agents reaching each other answer with each other's last render. Root's web proc serves the user agent's hiccup as html and Datastar patches, a malformed answer an error on that user's page; root runs no render. Opening an eval is an eval with origin :context the user agent sends the agent. What a person types is read by the user agent (seon.reader/words-and-forms) and sent to root or the agent the view is on. Deletes: seon.render.proc's user-walk and root-walk, its page passes over agents' branches, seon.root/opened!. Proofs: one pushed answer changes one component (measured in the lab 2026-11-09: 1.0 ms); a page open in two tabs is one render; the user agent's death leaves root and other users' pages up.

Experiment D, 2026-11-09 (Web, same demo root): the agents' renders the user agent holds, one ratom diffed by seon.ctx/changes (a sorted map by agent key, each a sorted map by component id), read in a component through seon.ratom/ref-view: no new code. One part changed reran that part and its agent's list (0.23 ms, 2 agents x 30 parts of about 400 bytes); a part added, the new part and its agent's list; a status moved on main, that agent's component alone; another agent's part, that agent's alone. Estimate: O(1) for the changed part plus a lookup per part of that one agent. Each answer's components need ids as keys (the key-id and data-rev of ``seon.repl/an-agents-process-renders-data-to-hiccup``)."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "one-tree-is-read-by-the-page-and-the-model"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-10: \"In the hiccup can we be selective in what we are rendering in full? Like we don't need to repeat the messages and evals in hiccup if we are doing them in assistant, user, tool calls. Can that be a [function-that-would-render-them-in-full args children] and have that not expand for the agent's context?\"; and, correcting the first plan: \"I don't want to change how the model is seeing it's evals as tool calls though. I'm talking about having the hiccup just have the vector to render that part and don't expand it to show everything all over again in antoher format but the agent still sees the full UI and they aren't in the dark with what the user is seeing\". Also: \"I want the styling in a separate attribute if possible and then we can strip it out\". And (2026-11-10, to the managing session): \"I want the core openai-compat format to be normal system, user, assistant and tool calls and that to be where the agent understands it's history -- not from the hiccup web render ... I think thats' the main duplication are of context that's huge and everything else we want expanded. But yeah remove style info by default from that and I want to make sure the agent knows what function was called for each part of the hiccup render so they can edit it to change the page.\"

What exists after stage 2b (task seon.render/a-model-render-draws-each-turns-diff): the agent's page is one walk (seon.render/walker), one tree of hiccup. The model reads its evals, messages and replies as tool calls and messages (each entity schema's :seon.render/openai-compat render, seon.ctx.wire/eval-msgs and the rest), which stays as it is. Of the page's tree the model reads one slice, the task panel (seon.render.ui/agent-parts), as each turn's <page from=\"seon\" turn=\"n\"> message: so the rest of what the person sees (where the conversation sits, the page around it) never reaches the model.

The change: the <page> message renders the agent's whole page tree, presentation stripped, and every conversation entity in it (an eval, a message, a reply: a component whose entity reaches the model as its own messages) stays the unexpanded call that renders it, [render-name {:key id}], never its content again. The agent sees the whole UI and where each of its evals sits, and nothing is said twice. Each part's render name is the head of its call (what (source f) reads), never a word of a class; the model's reading drops :class and :style whole, so style is the attributes alone (the page's Tailwind moving into each render's :seon.render/style is Web's, optional).

Who: Context the model's reading of the tree (seon.ctx.wire: page-parts-at reads the page's root render, the conversation's components left as calls); Web that each conversation entity is its own component in the tree (it is, seon.render.ui's conversation renders) and render names as heads.

Deletes: the slice (page-parts-at reading agent-parts alone); presentation-free's render-name-as-class.

Proofs: test/seon/recorded_requests.edn with each change named (the page message holds the conversation's calls where they sit); the tool calls and messages unchanged byte for byte; no eval's result text appears in a <page> message; an eval added in a turn is one call more in that turn's page edits."
